\Title {Learning Shared and Private Representations for Multi-omics Cancer Subtyping}
\Author {Reza Soumi}
\Copyright {\textcopyright \ 2026. This work is licensed under a Creative Commons "Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International" (BY-NC-SA 4.0) license.}
\CopyrightURL {https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/deed.en}
\Subject {Deep multi-omics models often learn shared and private latent codes for cancer subtyping and report strong unsupervised clustering metrics. This thesis asks whether those codes actually play their intended roles. On TCGA breast cancer data with multi-seed evaluation, MOCSS yields high integration NMI while private codes retain subtype structure and cross-view reconstruction gaps stay near zero. OMIDIENT appears better separated on private-subtype NMI, but probes indicate private-path collapse rather than verified privacy. Three methods are developed to enforce a clearer split: cross-view predictability constraints, joint reconstruction with VICReg independence, and spectral alignment of shared codes to a fused multi-omics patient graph. Results show that low private-subtype NMI is not a reliable disentanglement trophy, that Method 2 is preferable to the VICReg ablation of Method 1 for integration quality, and that Method 3 achieves the strongest cross-view shared-versus-private distinction among AE-private models while remaining competitive on clustering. }
\Keywords {multi-omics\sep cancer subtyping\sep shared representation\sep private representation\sep disentanglement}
